<header><p class="st-kicker">chiefdom · Mesoamerica and the Caribbean</p><h1 class="st-title">Ah Kin Chel</h1><p class="st-meta">1441 CE – 1542 CE (approx.) · capital Tecoh</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Maya province of north-central Yucatán, ruled by the priestly Chel lineage and including the old pilgrimage centre of Izamal.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>According to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-de-landa">Bishop Diego de Landa</a>, a priest of the Chel lineage at <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/league-of-mayapan">Mayapan</a> foresaw its fall and, after its destruction around 1441, founded a new realm. The province took his title, Ah Kin (priest) Chel, and its capital was at Tecoh.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Ah Kin Chel covered a large area of north-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yucatan-peninsula">Yucatán</a> reaching the coast, with salt beds and fishing grounds. It included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izamal">Izamal</a>, a pilgrimage centre of the god <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/itzamna">Itzamna</a> with massive ancient pyramids. Its ruler governed through the batabs of local towns, and the province maintained rivalries with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/sotuta">Sotuta</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/cupul">Cupul</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Ah Kin Chel submitted to the Spanish in the early 1540s and was absorbed into the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/province-of-yucatan">Province of Yucatán</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/franciscans">Franciscans</a> built a great convent on a pyramid at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/izamal">Izamal</a> in the 1550s.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/ah-kin-chel.jpg" alt="Ah Kin Chel" width="320" height="320"><figcaption>Image: Cacicazgos_mayas.jpg: Jaontiveros Mexico_States_blank_map.svg: Sémhur derivative work: ecelan (talk) · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cacicazgos_mayas_-_es.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1441 CE – 1542 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>chiefdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Tecoh</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Maya religion</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Yucatec Maya</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/mexico">Mexico</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Ah Kin Chel province</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=ah-kin-chel" aria-label="Ah Kin Chel on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="2272 1410 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Ah Kin Chel lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2592 1666L2591 1670M2592 1666L4910 989M2592 1666L2590 1658"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2591 1670h0M4910 989h0M2590 1658h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2592 1666h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2592 1666h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Tecoh. <a href="/atlas?year=1492&amp;focus=ah-kin-chel">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
